WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.110 --> 00:00:09.240
- Hello, and thanks so much for joining

00:00:09.240 --> 00:00:10.681
for this brief presentation

00:00:10.681 --> 00:00:14.160
on our experiences with
the EPI LAMP program.

00:00:14.160 --> 00:00:17.250
And specifically, how we
have adapted leadership

00:00:17.250 --> 00:00:19.870
and management training for
national immunization teams

00:00:19.870 --> 00:00:22.620
in the context of COVID-19.

00:00:22.620 --> 00:00:23.890
My name is Erika Linnander,

00:00:23.890 --> 00:00:25.260
I direct a group out of Yale

00:00:25.260 --> 00:00:27.440
called the Global Health
Leadership Initiative

00:00:27.440 --> 00:00:30.510
and I'm joined in this
experience by my colleagues

00:00:30.510 --> 00:00:31.970
in our partner organizations

00:00:31.970 --> 00:00:33.960
as part of the EPI LAMP consortium,

00:00:33.960 --> 00:00:35.010
including representatives

00:00:35.010 --> 00:00:37.630
from the University of Global
Health Equity in Rwanda,

00:00:37.630 --> 00:00:41.950
the University of Yaounde
in Cameroon, GAVI, PATH,

00:00:41.950 --> 00:00:45.543
and of course, our students
and alumni in the program.

00:00:46.540 --> 00:00:48.870
So the reason that I wanna talk with you,

00:00:48.870 --> 00:00:50.630
about the EPI LAMP experience

00:00:50.630 --> 00:00:53.040
and our adaptations in
the context of COVID,

00:00:53.040 --> 00:00:55.330
is that we know that
leadership and management

00:00:55.330 --> 00:00:57.550
are essential in the COVID response.

00:00:57.550 --> 00:00:58.720
So in times of crisis,

00:00:58.720 --> 00:01:00.860
we really need national immunization teams

00:01:00.860 --> 00:01:03.590
to be able to come
together, solve problems

00:01:03.590 --> 00:01:07.030
and move forward in a
coordinated national response.

00:01:07.030 --> 00:01:09.762
And yet at the same
time, we see investments

00:01:09.762 --> 00:01:13.132
in routine health system
strengthening being diverted

00:01:13.132 --> 00:01:15.683
into more acute COVID response.

00:01:15.683 --> 00:01:18.720
And, our old models of
training for leadership

00:01:18.720 --> 00:01:21.180
and management just aren't
viable in the context

00:01:21.180 --> 00:01:24.750
of COVID given restrictions
on travel and convening.

00:01:24.750 --> 00:01:25.960
And so the question becomes,

00:01:25.960 --> 00:01:29.560
how can we provide support for leadership

00:01:29.560 --> 00:01:32.840
and management in this new context?

00:01:32.840 --> 00:01:35.370
So just to provide a little bit background

00:01:35.370 --> 00:01:37.960
on what the EPI LAMP program is,

00:01:37.960 --> 00:01:40.730
EPI LAMP is a nine month
certificate program

00:01:40.730 --> 00:01:44.624
for natural immunization teams
in GAVI eligible countries.

00:01:44.624 --> 00:01:47.480
So we have participants come together

00:01:47.480 --> 00:01:51.270
as teams to build their
management and leadership capacity

00:01:51.270 --> 00:01:54.160
in the six areas shown
on the poster there,

00:01:54.160 --> 00:01:57.180
strategic problem solving,
leading effective teams,

00:01:57.180 --> 00:02:00.070
political engagement,
human resource management,

00:02:00.070 --> 00:02:03.160
financial management, and
supply chain management.

00:02:03.160 --> 00:02:05.520
We've been delivering
the program since 2018.

00:02:05.520 --> 00:02:10.300
We're now offering the program
for cohorts four and five.

00:02:10.300 --> 00:02:13.860
So we've reached 54 alumni
and 49 current delegates

00:02:13.860 --> 00:02:17.130
across 27 GAVI eligible countries

00:02:17.130 --> 00:02:21.825
in French and English
speaking, Africa and Asia.

00:02:21.825 --> 00:02:24.820
Obviously like most of us we were forced

00:02:24.820 --> 00:02:28.230
to adapt the program in
the context of COVID 19,

00:02:28.230 --> 00:02:29.390
and I wanna tell you a little bit

00:02:29.390 --> 00:02:32.610
about how we've done that
and our results to date.

00:02:32.610 --> 00:02:35.960
So if you zip over to the
right-hand side of the poster,

00:02:35.960 --> 00:02:38.990
you see, we used two
methods for adaptation.

00:02:38.990 --> 00:02:40.000
The first thing we did,

00:02:40.000 --> 00:02:42.500
is we worked with each
participating country

00:02:42.500 --> 00:02:44.870
to undergo a structured risk assessment

00:02:44.870 --> 00:02:47.650
to understand how were
they working remotely.

00:02:47.650 --> 00:02:49.130
Were they working remotely?

00:02:49.130 --> 00:02:50.460
How was it going?

00:02:50.460 --> 00:02:51.950
Any lessons learned there?

00:02:51.950 --> 00:02:55.122
What was their internet
connectivity and cost like?

00:02:55.122 --> 00:02:57.660
Would they be able to
participate in the program,

00:02:57.660 --> 00:02:59.710
given competing priorities?

00:02:59.710 --> 00:03:02.570
So what would the program have
to look like to be on demand

00:03:02.570 --> 00:03:06.810
and flexible and how might
they like to collaborate?

00:03:06.810 --> 00:03:10.010
We combined that
country-specific risk assessment

00:03:10.010 --> 00:03:12.510
with a review of the literature,

00:03:12.510 --> 00:03:13.357
the peer reviewed literature

00:03:13.357 --> 00:03:16.100
and the gray literature
around best practices

00:03:16.100 --> 00:03:19.193
for online education for professionals.

00:03:20.050 --> 00:03:22.600
And as a result of those two inputs,

00:03:22.600 --> 00:03:24.400
the country specific assessment

00:03:24.400 --> 00:03:27.700
and then the kind of systematic
review of best practices,

00:03:27.700 --> 00:03:29.590
we came up with five adaptations

00:03:29.590 --> 00:03:31.104
to the EPI LAMP experience

00:03:31.104 --> 00:03:32.960
that I wanna talk with you about.

00:03:32.960 --> 00:03:34.524
The first thing that we came up with,

00:03:34.524 --> 00:03:36.310
with a framework,

00:03:36.310 --> 00:03:39.900
we came up with a way of
approaching in the creation

00:03:39.900 --> 00:03:41.810
of a professional learning community,

00:03:41.810 --> 00:03:42.820
that was evidence-based.

00:03:42.820 --> 00:03:44.220
So we could have confidence,

00:03:44.220 --> 00:03:45.990
and our participants could have confidence

00:03:45.990 --> 00:03:48.580
that we were attending to
all the different parts

00:03:48.580 --> 00:03:51.100
of curricular approaches
that would be needed

00:03:51.100 --> 00:03:52.523
for them to be successful.

00:03:53.400 --> 00:03:56.750
We also shifted away
from in-person convenings

00:03:56.750 --> 00:03:59.080
and instead moved to increase investment

00:03:59.080 --> 00:04:01.560
in some small group facilitation online,

00:04:01.560 --> 00:04:03.600
meaning a lots of breakout rooms,

00:04:03.600 --> 00:04:05.760
investment in facilitators
in those breakout rooms,

00:04:05.760 --> 00:04:08.590
they could use Miro and
other knowledge capture tools

00:04:08.590 --> 00:04:10.280
to make sure that these sessions

00:04:10.280 --> 00:04:13.420
were really active and engaging.

00:04:13.420 --> 00:04:17.060
We updated the curriculum with
COVID specific applications.

00:04:17.060 --> 00:04:19.770
So, still teaching the real essentials

00:04:19.770 --> 00:04:21.170
of management and leadership,

00:04:21.170 --> 00:04:23.090
then making sure that
they were always focused

00:04:23.090 --> 00:04:26.020
on the practical challenges
that teams were facing

00:04:26.020 --> 00:04:29.360
in the context of the COVID response.

00:04:29.360 --> 00:04:33.030
We broadened our communication
channels to include Slack

00:04:33.030 --> 00:04:35.860
and WhatsApp so that we're
able to reach delegates,

00:04:35.860 --> 00:04:38.470
to reach participants on multiple channels

00:04:38.470 --> 00:04:39.890
throughout the day, throughout the week,

00:04:39.890 --> 00:04:41.480
throughout the program.

00:04:41.480 --> 00:04:43.640
And finally, we found mechanisms

00:04:43.640 --> 00:04:46.720
to get participants
cash for mobile access.

00:04:46.720 --> 00:04:49.310
So across our participating countries,

00:04:49.310 --> 00:04:51.510
we found that actually
the internet was okay,

00:04:51.510 --> 00:04:52.640
it was just expensive.

00:04:52.640 --> 00:04:55.830
And so we needed, if we wanted
participants to come online,

00:04:55.830 --> 00:04:58.280
turn on their videos,
participate actively,

00:04:58.280 --> 00:04:59.560
we needed to make sure

00:04:59.560 --> 00:05:01.420
that they had the financial compensation

00:05:01.420 --> 00:05:04.373
to buy that airtime and
turn their videos on.

00:05:05.290 --> 00:05:07.610
We're just over halfway
through the program now,

00:05:07.610 --> 00:05:10.530
through cohorts four and
five with this new model.

00:05:10.530 --> 00:05:13.140
And I wanna share a little
bit about results to date.

00:05:13.140 --> 00:05:15.370
First of all, as you see
in that middle panel there,

00:05:15.370 --> 00:05:17.120
we're having 88 participation

00:05:17.120 --> 00:05:19.220
in our bi-weekly live sessions.

00:05:19.220 --> 00:05:21.820
That means that despite
their busy schedules,

00:05:21.820 --> 00:05:24.432
despite the challenges
of internet connectivity,

00:05:24.432 --> 00:05:28.014
participants are coming
online and participating.

00:05:28.014 --> 00:05:30.420
We think this is because the sessions

00:05:30.420 --> 00:05:31.710
are highly interactive,

00:05:31.710 --> 00:05:33.180
because the content is relevant

00:05:33.180 --> 00:05:34.050
and because they're learning

00:05:34.050 --> 00:05:36.263
from each other in those sessions.

00:05:37.290 --> 00:05:39.500
We've also seen that
the demand for coaching,

00:05:39.500 --> 00:05:41.950
we provide coaching to
each one of the teams

00:05:41.950 --> 00:05:44.050
and the demand for
those coaching sessions,

00:05:44.050 --> 00:05:47.530
is twice what it has been in past cohorts.

00:05:47.530 --> 00:05:50.640
So again, even though these
participants are very busy

00:05:50.640 --> 00:05:51.900
in the context of COVID,

00:05:51.900 --> 00:05:54.142
we're seeing that they
actually want more coaching,

00:05:54.142 --> 00:05:57.320
again, highlighting the
relevance of the program.

00:05:57.320 --> 00:06:00.203
And because of this new model where things

00:06:00.203 --> 00:06:01.036
are better integrated,

00:06:01.036 --> 00:06:02.763
and we're being much more responsive,

00:06:02.763 --> 00:06:06.431
we're seeing better progress
on their breakthrough projects

00:06:06.431 --> 00:06:10.080
now halfway through the program
compared to prior cohorts.

00:06:10.080 --> 00:06:12.590
So this integrated virtual model

00:06:12.590 --> 00:06:15.470
of delivery is actually
delivering better results

00:06:15.470 --> 00:06:18.201
than the old model of
bringing participants together

00:06:18.201 --> 00:06:21.113
for periodic in-person trainings.

00:06:22.090 --> 00:06:22.923
So what does this mean?

00:06:22.923 --> 00:06:24.760
What do we take from this experience?

00:06:24.760 --> 00:06:27.500
First of all, national
immunization professionals

00:06:27.500 --> 00:06:29.580
are demanding leadership support

00:06:29.580 --> 00:06:34.580
despite the competing challenges
associated with COVID-19.

00:06:34.720 --> 00:06:36.350
So we thought they'd be too busy.

00:06:36.350 --> 00:06:37.620
We thought they'd be distracted,

00:06:37.620 --> 00:06:38.840
but instead they're telling us,

00:06:38.840 --> 00:06:42.030
no, we need leadership support now,

00:06:42.030 --> 00:06:44.831
do not cancel this thing.

00:06:44.831 --> 00:06:46.180
The second implication

00:06:46.180 --> 00:06:48.960
that we're seeing is that
through systematic adaptation

00:06:48.960 --> 00:06:51.420
using the methods that
I've described earlier,

00:06:51.420 --> 00:06:55.090
virtual models can be accessible,
engaging, and responsive.

00:06:55.090 --> 00:06:57.430
So the virtual stuff can work

00:06:57.430 --> 00:07:00.350
as long as you go about it systematically

00:07:00.350 --> 00:07:02.060
and based on evidence.

00:07:02.060 --> 00:07:03.690
And lastly, I think we're seeing

00:07:03.690 --> 00:07:07.120
from our participants, a call
for leadership development

00:07:07.120 --> 00:07:09.780
to remain part and integral part

00:07:09.780 --> 00:07:12.953
of national and global
response and recovery.

00:07:13.790 --> 00:07:15.720
Thanks for taking the time to listen

00:07:15.720 --> 00:07:18.860
and learn a little bit more
about the EPI LAMP program.

00:07:18.860 --> 00:07:19.920
If you wanna learn even more,

00:07:19.920 --> 00:07:22.530
scan the QR code up in
the top left corner,

00:07:22.530 --> 00:07:23.733
that will take you to our website.

00:07:23.733 --> 00:07:25.560
That'll give you a little
bit more background

00:07:25.560 --> 00:07:28.310
on the program as well as
some mobile friendly content.

00:07:28.310 --> 00:07:29.620
So you don't have to squint

00:07:29.620 --> 00:07:32.020
at this tiny little PDF poster.

00:07:32.020 --> 00:07:32.853
Thanks again.

